 <h2 id="pattern">The traffic pattern</h2>
 <p>The traffic pattern organises arrivals and departures around a runway. It is a rectangle with turns to the left unless the airport shows right turns. <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-1')}</a> <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-3')}</a> Its legs are: <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-3')}</a> <a href="#source-pcg">{pcg('Traffic pattern')}</a></p>
 <ol>
  <li><strong>Departure leg</strong>: straight ahead along the extended runway centerline after takeoff, until at least 1/2 mile beyond the runway end and within 300 ft of pattern altitude. An <strong>upwind leg</strong> extends it where needed for separation, spacing or sequencing.</li>
  <li><strong>Crosswind leg</strong>: at right angles to the landing runway, off its takeoff end. Turn onto it beyond the departure end of the runway, within 300 ft of pattern altitude.</li>
  <li><strong>Downwind leg</strong>: parallel to the landing runway, in the opposite direction to landing.</li>
  <li><strong>Base leg</strong>: at right angles to the landing runway, off its approach end, from the downwind leg to the extended centerline.</li>
  <li><strong>Final approach</strong>: along the extended runway centerline, in the direction of landing, from the base leg to the runway. Complete the turn to final at least 1/4 mile from the runway.</li>
 </ol>
 <p><strong>Altitude.</strong> Propeller-driven aircraft enter the pattern at 1,000 ft above ground level (AGL); large and turbine-powered aircraft at not less than 1,500 ft AGL or 500 ft above the established pattern altitude. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-3')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Spacing and turns.</strong> The downwind leg is flown approximately 1/2 to 1 mile out from the landing runway. The turn to base starts at a point approximately 45° from the approach end of the runway. <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-3')}</a> When aircraft are approaching to land, the one at the lower altitude has the right-of-way, but it should not use that to cut in front of or overtake an aircraft already on final. <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-4')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Entering.</strong> "Entry to the downwind leg should be at a 45-degree angle abeam the midpoint of the runway to be used for landing." <a href="#source-ac">{ac('11.3')}</a> From the upwind side, the Airplane Flying Handbook describes crossing over midfield at least 500 ft above pattern altitude, descending to pattern altitude about 2 miles out, and turning to enter at 45°. <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-4')}</a> The FAA discourages VFR straight-in approaches because they raise the risk of a midair collision. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('9.11.1')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Leaving.</strong> Continue straight out or exit with a 45° turn, beyond the departure end of the runway after reaching pattern altitude. <a href="#source-ac">{ac('11.8')}</a> <a href="#source-afh">{afh('p. 8-4')}</a></p>
