 <h2 id="overhead">The overhead maneuver</h2>
 <p>At airports where aircraft have an operational need for it, often military fields, aircraft can join the pattern with an <strong>overhead maneuver</strong> instead of a rectangular entry. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('3-10-12')}</a> The Pilot/Controller Glossary defines it as "A series of predetermined maneuvers prescribed for aircraft (often in formation)" for entry into the visual flight rules (VFR) traffic pattern and to proceed to a landing. It is not an instrument (IFR) approach procedure: an aircraft flying it is VFR, and its IFR flight plan is cancelled at the "initial point" on the initial approach. <a href="#source-pcg">{pcg('Overhead maneuver')}</a> The pattern usually specifies: <a href="#source-pcg">{pcg('Overhead maneuver')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li>the radio contact required of the pilot, and the speed to be maintained;</li>
  <li>an initial approach 3 to 5 miles in length;</li>
  <li>an elliptical pattern consisting of two 180° turns;</li>
  <li>a break point at which the first 180° turn is started, and the direction of turns;</li>
  <li>an altitude at least 500 ft above the conventional pattern;</li>
  <li>a roll-out on final approach not less than 1/4 mile from the landing threshold and not less than 300 ft above the ground.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>Overhead patterns are developed at airports where aircraft have an operational need for them, and a standard overhead pattern does not rule out the conventional rectangular pattern. If the break point is not standard, the tower names it, for example "break at midfield". <a href="#source-atc">{atc('3-10-12')}</a></p>
