 <h2 id="ground">Moving on the ground</h2>
 <p>At an airport with an operating tower, approval is needed before moving an aircraft or vehicle onto the movement area, and a clearance is needed before taxiing on a runway, taking off, landing or crossing any runway. ATC issues an explicit clearance for every runway crossing, and a taxi instruction to the departure runway is not a clearance to enter or cross it. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-18')}</a> Pilots unfamiliar with an airport can ask for progressive taxi instructions, and should remember that jet blast, prop wash and rotor wash can damage aircraft and equipment close behind. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-18')}</a> How those calls are worded belongs to Radio Communications.</p>
 <p>Towers control aircraft, vehicles and people without radios, or whose radio contact cannot be established, with a light gun: an intense narrow beam of a selected colour. Light signals can only approve or disapprove what the pilot is expected to do, and a pilot may not be looking at the tower when a signal is sent. <a href="#source-aim4">{aim4('4-3-13')}</a></p>
