 <h2 id="terms">Airfield terms</h2>
 <dl class="jargon">
  <div><dt>Runway designator</dt><dd>The whole number nearest one-tenth of a runway's magnetic direction, with L, C or R for parallels.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Threshold</dt><dd>The beginning of that portion of the runway usable for landing.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Displaced threshold</dt><dd>A threshold not at the beginning of the pavement; the pavement before it may be used for takeoff and for landing from the other direction.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Aiming point</dt><dd>Two broad white stripes either side of the centerline, about 1,000 ft from the landing threshold.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Touchdown zone markings</dt><dd>Pairs of one, two and three bars coding distance from the threshold in 500 ft steps.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Holding position marking</dt><dd>Four yellow lines across a taxiway, two solid and two dashed; aircraft hold on the solid side.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Mandatory instruction sign</dt><dd>A red sign with a white inscription, marking a runway or critical area entrance or a no-entry area.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Location sign</dt><dd>A black sign with a yellow inscription and border, telling you which taxiway or runway you are on.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Caution zone</dt><dd>The last 2,000 ft, or half the runway if less, of an instrument runway, where edge lights are yellow.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>PAPI</dt><dd>A row of two or four red-and-white light units beside the runway showing the approach angle.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Crosswind component</dt><dd>The part of the wind blowing across the runway: wind speed times the sine of the angle between wind and runway.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Tetrahedron</dt><dd>A landing direction indicator whose small end points in the direction of landing.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Downwind leg</dt><dd>The pattern leg parallel to the landing runway, flown opposite to the landing direction.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Base leg</dt><dd>The pattern leg at right angles to the runway off its approach end, from downwind to the extended centerline.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Upwind leg</dt><dd>An extension of the departure leg used for separation, spacing or sequencing.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Break</dt><dd>The point in an overhead maneuver where the first 180° turn begins.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Initial</dt><dd>The initial approach of an overhead maneuver, 3 to 5 miles long, flown toward the break.</dd></div>
 </dl>
