 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>A runway's centerline points 004° magnetic. What is it numbered, and what is its other end?</summary><p>Runway 36, since 0.4 rounds to 0 and north is written 36. The other end points 184°: runway 18.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are approaching three parallel runways numbered 27. Which one is on your left?</summary><p>27L. Seen from the other end it is 9R.</p></details>
 <details><summary>A 100 ft wide runway: how many threshold stripes?</summary><p>Eight.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are taxiing toward a runway and see two solid and two dashed yellow lines. Which side must you stop on?</summary><p>The side with the solid lines, which is yours as you approach from the taxiway. Do not cross without a clearance.</p></details>
 <details><summary>After landing you stop with your tail just over the dashed lines. Are you clear of the runway?</summary><p>No. All parts of the aircraft must cross the holding position marking, the solid lines included, before you are clear.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The sign beside you is black with a yellow "B" and a yellow border. What does it tell you?</summary><p>You are on taxiway B. It is a location sign.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The edge lights ahead have turned yellow. What does that mean?</summary><p>You are in the caution zone of an instrument runway: the last 2,000 ft, or half the runway if that is less.</p></details>
 <details><summary>On approach, the PAPI shows three red and one white. What do you do?</summary><p>You are slightly low; reduce your rate of descent to regain two white and two red.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Wind 240° at 15 kt, runways 27 and 18. Which is in use, and how much crosswind?</summary><p>Runway 27, 30° off the wind (runway 18 is 60° off). Crosswind 15 × sin 30° = 7.5 kt from the left.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Where and how do you join the pattern?</summary><p>At pattern altitude, at 45° to the downwind leg, abeam the runway midpoint.</p></details>
 <details><summary>An overhead pattern's conventional pattern altitude is 1,000 ft AGL. What is the lowest altitude for the overhead?</summary><p>1,500 ft AGL: at least 500 ft above the conventional pattern.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are taxiing without a radio and the tower shows you a flashing green. What does it mean?</summary><p>Cleared for taxi. A steady green would clear you for takeoff.</p></details>

